<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>Arzoo Samarth Jagnani v. Smarth Vimal Jagnani &amp; Ors., Crl.A. No. 320/2025</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>Sri Jayaprakash A., LXVI Addl. City Civil &amp; Sessions Judge, Bengaluru City (DV appellate court)</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Decided</th><td>3 November 2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Provisions</th><td>Sections 3, 12, 18, 19, 20, 22 and 29, DV Act, 2005; Section 65-B, Evidence Act</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Appeal allowed; dismissal set aside; matter remitted for fresh consideration</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The parties married in Mumbai in June 2014. The wife&rsquo;s Section 12 petition alleged taunts over wedding expenditure said to run to about Rs 1.27 crore, physical intimidation, verbal and emotional abuse, restrictions on her career, retention of stridhan and financial deprivation, and asserted that she was forced out of the matrimonial home in July 2015. The husband and his family denied the allegations entirely, contending that she left of her own accord and that the DV petition was a pressure tactic in the parties&rsquo; pending matrimonial litigation.</p>
<p>The Magistrate dismissed the petition in January 2025. The wife appealed under Section 29 of the Act.</p>

<h2>Section 3 supplies the yardstick</h2>
<p>The appellate court&rsquo;s central criticism of the trial order was that it tested the wife&rsquo;s allegations against the standards of matrimonial cruelty familiar from divorce law, rather than against the statutory definition. Section 3 of the DV Act defines domestic violence through specific categories — physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and economic abuse — and the conduct alleged must be measured against those categories, cumulatively rather than incident by incident.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;The determination of domestic violence must be made strictly in accordance with Section 3 of the D.V. Act.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>The court drew on the settled Supreme Court line on the Act&rsquo;s protective architecture, including <em>Hiral P. Harsora</em>, <em>Prabha Tyagi</em>, <em>V.D. Bhanot</em>, <em>Satish Chander Ahuja</em> and <em>Krishna Bhattacharjee</em>.</p>

<h2>Secret recordings: admissibility versus weight</h2>
<p>The wife relied on audio recordings made without the husband&rsquo;s knowledge. The appellate court held that covert recording does not by itself render the material inadmissible, but the recordings, being electronic records, must be proved in the manner required by Section 65-B of the Evidence Act, in line with <em>Anvar P.V.</em> and <em>Arjun Panditrao Khotkar</em>. The court was equally clear about the converse:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;A recording which is inadmissible cannot be relied upon to establish an allegation.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>The discipline the judgment asks for is the separation of two questions that are often blurred in practice: whether the recording can be looked at, and how much it proves once it is.</p>

<h2>Economic abuse and the finances of both sides</h2>
<p>The Magistrate had treated the wife&rsquo;s employment history as answering the economic-abuse allegation. The appellate court disagreed: employment does not conclusively negate economic abuse, just as the husband&rsquo;s income does not automatically entitle the aggrieved person to monetary relief. Both parties&rsquo; financial positions require proper assessment, and the court permitted updated affidavits of assets of the kind standardised in <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For aggrieved persons.</strong> Plead the conduct by Section 3 category — physical, verbal, emotional, economic — rather than as an undifferentiated narrative of cruelty. The statutory categories are the yardstick the court must apply.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For respondents.</strong> An order that tests DV allegations on divorce-law cruelty standards is vulnerable in appeal either way. The remedy under Section 29 reaches errors of legal approach, not only outcomes.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For evidence strategy.</strong> Recordings need a Section 65-B foundation. A certificate and proper proof of the device and transfer chain should be in place before the recording is pressed in argument.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Although the decision comes from a Bengaluru appellate court, the propositions it applies are drawn from Supreme Court authority that binds proceedings everywhere, including before the Mahila Courts and Sessions Courts of Delhi. DV petitions are frequently fought, and sometimes dismissed, on an implicit comparison with the high threshold of matrimonial cruelty. The judgment is a useful, recent reminder that the DV Act carries its own definitions, its own reliefs under Sections 18 to 22, and its own evidentiary discipline for electronic records.</p>
